==Appendix A: Cost Model Calculations for Multiply and Divide==

Multiplication and division require multiple passes over the operands, meaning a cost proportional to the square of the lengths involved, and the word size used for that iteration makes a difference.  We assume 8 bytes (64 bits) at a time are evaluated, and the ability to multiply two 64-bit numbers and receive a 128-bit result, and divide a 128-bit number by a 64-bit number to receive a 64-bit quotient and remainder.  This is true on modern 64-bit CPUs (sometimes using multiple instructions).

===Multiplication Cost===

With <code>n</code> and <code>m</code> as defined with the OP_MUL cost above, <code>MUL(n, m)</code> charges the complete multiplication of the operands, modeled as schoolbook multiplication: one row per 64-bit word of the shorter operand, each multiplying the words of the longer operand by that word and adding the row into the product.  Its four coefficients are a constant, a pass over the longer operand (<code>W(n)</code>), each row's fixed overhead (<code>W(m)</code>) and the word products (<code>W(n) W(m)</code>).  The shorter operand sets the rows whichever operand is on top, so no asymmetry depends on stack position.

The product's WRITE covers its full span, <code>W(n) + W(m)</code> bytes, whatever the trimmed result length.

===Division Cost===

<code>n</code> and <code>m</code> are defined with the OP_DIV cost above.  <code>DIV(n, m)</code> charges rows over the divisor's words: one for each word of <code>Q(n, m)</code> from [[bip-0440.mediawiki|BIP440]], by which the dividend exceeds the divisor, plus a fixed few (a possible quotient word and the dividend word that normalizing the divisor can add), which its constant and <code>W(m)</code> terms pay for.  A dividend no longer than the divisor pays only those, although it only needs a comparison of the operands' lengths; the over-charge keeps one formula for every operand size.  <code>n</code> and <code>m</code> are trimmed lengths because arithmetic operands may carry zero padding (bit and byte operations do not normalize), and padding changes neither the quotient nor the work.  Encoded lengths would let a padded divisor reduce <code>Q(n, m)</code> to zero while the division still performs one word operation per quotient word and divisor word.  Finding the trimmed lengths is a part of reading each operand, charged as <code>READ(length(A)) + READ(length(B))</code>.  The <code>Q(n, m)</code> term covers each row's quotient-digit estimate and correction, which does not shrink with the divisor; without it, a long dividend and a one- or two-word divisor are undercharged.  OP_MOD also shifts the remainder back after normalization, which is not charged separately.
